From 47906b39d16924ff8418f1bb28b2b972cc12ad68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:25:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] [Core][KV Offload] Free the CPU offload region for a
 process checkpoint

/checkpoint/prepare resets the CPU offloading connector's index but
leaves its region resident and registered with CUDA, so a process
checkpoint carries --kv-offloading-size bytes of dead data. With a
128 GiB region a CRIU image grew from 11.5 GB to 139 GB and restoring
it took longer than a cold boot.

Preparing now unregisters the region on every rank, waits for all ranks
at the worker barrier, and frees the pages with MADV_REMOVE. Pages that
another rank still has registered would stay allocated, hence the
barrier. The mappings stay, so no pointer changes. Restoring pre-faults
each rank's share and registers it again, and fails if the registration
does not come back, because the transfer kernels dereference the host
pointers.

The engine asks for the release explicitly, after it has reset the
connector. Worker.checkpoint_prepare is also called by
CUDACheckpointExecutor during a sleep that may keep the connector's
contents, so it does not release by default.

Only the shared region of the CPU spec is released. The tiering spec
and SimpleCPUOffloadConnector keep their memory.

Signed-off-by: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
(cherry picked from commit 1a5949a09097427a24ecad987ab39e24ad645f23)
